title: _cli_transport deadlocks forever when git writes more than 64 KiB to stderr
updated: 2026-08-09T17:53:35Z
---

`lib/App/karr/Git.pm:438-439`:

    my $out = <$out_fh>;   # slurps stdout to EOF FIRST
    $err = <$err_fh>;

Classic single-threaded open3 ordering bug. Once the child has written 64 KiB to
stderr the pipe buffer is full and the child blocks; it therefore never exits and
never closes stdout, while the parent is still blocked reading stdout. Neither
side moves again. There is no timeout anywhere in this path.

Reproduced with real git — bare origin plus a clone with 800 diverged
`refs/karr/tasks/N/data` refs:

    git fetch origin 'refs/karr/*:refs/karr/*' 2>&1 >/dev/null | wc -c   -> 75220
    timeout 15 perl -e '...->_cli_transport("fetch","origin",[...])'
    -> exit 124 (hung)

Measured threshold: 600 rejected refs still complete, 700 hang (~64 KiB).

Not a theoretical board size: the pull-refspec bug guarantees that **every** ref
on a diverged board is rejected, and each rejection is one stderr line. A board
of ~700 tasks on the ssh/ProxyCommand transport is enough. It can also hang
inside `bin/karr`'s END flush, i.e. after the command has already printed its
result and the user believes it finished.

Fix direction: read both handles without blocking (IO::Select, or
`IPC::Run3`/`Capture::Tiny` if a dependency is acceptable), and put a timeout on
the transport so an unattended agent cannot hang forever.
